Axn
  subfield defaults
    subfield defaults with blank values
      with non-blank default and allow_blank: false
        when subfield is missing
          applies default and {:success=>true, :description=>"passes validation"}
        when subfield is explicitly nil
          applies default and {:success=>true, :description=>"passes validation"}
        when subfield has blank string value
          preserves existing blank value and {:success=>false, :description=>"fails validation"}
        when subfield has non-blank value
          preserves existing value and passes validation
  contradiction rejections (PRO-2877)
    model: subfield via a dotted on:
      does not raise for a model: subfield reached via a dotted on:
    verify-before-commit
      does not commit the rejected subfields when the declaration error is rescued
      does not leave an orphaned reader when the declaration error is rescued
    model: TOP-LEVEL batch naming its own <field>_id companion (parity with the subfield guard)
      raises at declaration, either order
    model: subfield batch naming its own <field>_id companion
      raises: model: applies to every field, so the explicit :<field>_id is a broken second model, either order
  value-level default fallback (PRO-2889)
    falls back to the default when the parent record's attribute is nil
    falls back when the id-resolved record's attribute is nil
    falls back when the model parent is omitted entirely
    falls back when the present parent cannot answer the key
    with a record-supplying default on a model subfield
      resolves the defaulted record when the chain is refused
    when the parent answers the key
      prefers a present attribute over the default
      falls back when the attribute is nil
    with a REQUIRED defaulted subfield under a nil-tolerant model parent (value-level defaults)
      succeeds on omission: the default satisfies validation and the parent stays nil
      succeeds on explicit nil
      still reads the record's value when id-resolved
      rejects a BLANK default a presence validator rejects at declaration (the tolerance is dead)
    with a defaulted subfield reached via a dotted on: under a refused chain
      validates the fallback value
    write-path behavior (PRO-2889)
      never mutates a caller-supplied record with a default
      evaluates a Proc default exactly once when the write chain is refused
      resolves a Proc default exactly once per call (memoized across validators)
      still materializes fully-object-shaped chains over an explicit nil (unchanged)
    with a sibling <field>_id default reaching model subfield resolution (PRO-2889)
      resolves the record via the sibling id default when the object-shaped chain omits the id
      resolves the record via the sibling id's VALUE-LEVEL default when the write chain is refused
      does NOT override a present raw id with the sibling default (a failed lookup stays nil)
    with a transformed sibling <field>_id feeding a STRICT finder (present id, PRO-2910)
      resolves the record from a coerced sibling id (string wire value → integer finder key)
      resolves the record from a preprocessed sibling id
      resolves via a coerced sibling id under coerce_input_types (no per-field coerce:)
      prefers the id route declared BESIDE the model over a differently-transformed merged route
      resolves nil when a present sibling id's preprocess maps it to nil (tolerant finder, never raw)
      does not fall through to another merged route when the own route maps a PRESENT id to nil
      does NOT rescue an absent id through an aliased route on another spelling
      rescues an absent id through the route that OWNS the canonical <field>_id reader
      prefers the model's OWN route over the canonically-named one
    an ineligible id route supplies nothing at all (PRO-3068)
      looks the record up by the caller's RAW token, not the ineligible route's transform
      withdraws the declaration-time rescue credit, raising the dead-tolerance error
      still credits the byte-identical shape whose id route owns the canonical reader name
    with a model subfield reached via a dotted on: and a sibling id (PRO-2889)
      resolves the record via the sibling id's VALUE-LEVEL default when the write chain is refused (C7)
      resolves via the wire write-back when the object-shaped chain omits the id (regression)
      skips the id default when the sibling record is already present (C8)
      loads cleanly: the sibling id credits the required descendant's rescue (declaration side)
    resolve_value cache is scoped to the settled pipeline (PRO-2889)
      reads the settled wire value, not a value cached before preprocess/defaults settled
    a merged nil-tolerant non-model route at a sibling-id-rescued node (PRO-2889)
      runs after loading cleanly: the model route resolves via the sibling id, non-model nil tolerated
    a leaf default must not clobber a model-routed wire key (PRO-2889)
      resolves the record via the sibling id, not the non-model route's written default
    an id-sibling default must not manufacture a model-consistency mismatch (PRO-2889)
      honors a present sibling record over axn's own id default
      still supplies the id default as a lookup token when the record is absent
    subfield model-consistency compares against the TRANSFORMED sibling id (PRO-2910)
      does NOT fabricate a conflict when a present record matches the id only after preprocess:
      still raises when the present record disagrees with the transformed id
      compares against the model's OWN-route id in a merged node (not declaration-order first)
    a model whose sibling <field>_id opts into method_call: on a PORO/Data parent (PRO-2910)
      resolves the record via the method_call sibling id (finder path)
      compares a present record against the method_call sibling id (consistency path)
      dispatches a method_call sibling id reader at most once (finder + reader share one read)
      reads a method_call RECORD key at most once when deriving from the implicit id (no declared id)
    a stateful sibling <field>_id transform runs at most once (model declared BEFORE its id, PRO-2910)
      resolves the record and the reader from a SINGLE preprocess run (they agree)
    the auto <field>_id companion agrees with the transformed lookup when the id is aliased (PRO-2910)
      returns the transformed id from the subfield companion, matching the resolved record
    subfield reader memos are cleared at the pipeline boundary (PRO-2889)
      validates the settled parent value, not a value memoized before the parent was rewritten
      re-resolves a model subfield record against the rewritten parent id
      clears the raw-extract memo, so a PRE-pipeline read doesn't pin a stale subfield wire value (PRO-2910)
    two defaults on one sibling-id wire node resolve through the model's own route (PRO-3068)
      resolves a merged doubly-defaulted id through the model's own route
    a descendant anchored on an aliased merged route resolves through THAT route (PRO-2926)
      reads the leaf through the anchoring route's reader, honoring its default

